# 🎫️26/08/10/ARTIFACT-SYSTEM-OVERHAUL-REAL-CODECS-RUNTIME-REUSE-EVOLUTION FG3: `stdio.ply`'s Pack
# facet (post-SEMIO-envelope-unwrap payload) is `encode_ply_with_format(self, self.format)`'s
# real on-disk bytes — genuinely one of THREE variants (`ascii` / `binary_little_endian` /
# `binary_big_endian`), see `../../🦀️.rs` (fixed this wave — `encode_pack_with`
# previously called the ascii-forcing `encode_ply(self)` unconditionally, silently discarding
# `self.format` on every Pack round-trip; see this wave's own report).
#
# THIS IS THE DOCUMENTED, PRE-APPROVED M2 SCOPE BOUNDARY (`📖️grammar-recipe.md` §6, restated
# verbatim in this FG3 brief, which explicitly names `ply` alongside `pdf/1.7` for this EXACT
# reason): "PLY's per-file schema selecting its own binary field list from an ASCII header —
# deferred. Document these fields as bounded-but-schema-external; real semantic resolution stays
# in Rust." Verified live, not assumed, before falling back to this treatment:
#   - `Prim`'s real primitive set (`🗣️dsl/📖️grammar/🦀️.rs`) has NO "scan forward until
#     this LITERAL MULTI-BYTE STRING" construct — `MarkerScan(u8)` only scans past a run of ONE
#     repeated fill byte then reads ONE discriminator byte (JPG's `0xFF...0xFF <code>` shape);
#     `backward <name> magic 0x...` only locates a FIXED-width magic by scanning BACKWARD from
#     EOF (ZIP's EOCD). Neither can locate the literal ASCII line `end_header\n` that terminates
#     PLY's header, which is otherwise unboundedly long (arbitrary element/property/comment
#     count, arbitrary name lengths).
#   - No construct lets a value the GRAMMAR side already parsed (element/property declarations,
#     read from ASCII TEXT) parameterize a PROTOCOL field's width/count/presence — `Count::Field`
#     only reaches an earlier field in the SAME protocol walk's own local env, never across
#     dialects. This is the identical root cause PDF 1.7's own sibling protocol file documents
#     for its `/W`-array xref-row-width gap.
#   - `Prim::Ref` to a struct/enum unconditionally errors at walk time regardless
#     (`protocol-prim-ref-recursion`) — even if the header's END could be located, a per-file
#     dynamic property LIST (arbitrary count of arbitrarily-typed columns) has no protocol-
#     description shape at all; see `protocol-array-of-records`'s own generalization of this gap.
#
# What IS genuinely universal, byte-checked, author-time-fixed across EVERY real `.ply` file
# regardless of which of the 3 format variants or what schema it declares: the 4-byte magic
# `"ply\n"` (`split_header`'s own `b"end_header"` scan starts searching from byte 0;
# `header_text`'s own first `out.push_str("ply\n")` — ../../⚙️engine/🦀️.rs). Modeled as a
# real, individually walked+validated field; everything past it (the `format`/`comment`/
# `element`/`property` header lines, the `end_header\n` terminator, and the body — ASCII numeric
# text OR one of the two binary cell layouts, depending on the header's own `format` line) is ONE
# opaque trailing chain, honestly bounded but schema-external — same treatment DWG ac1024's own
# sibling protocol file gives its irreducible encrypted+compressed pipeline (`📖️grammar-recipe.md`
# §6's own DWG bullet).
#
# `framing record` (NOT `framing magic ...`) deliberately, matching DWG ac1018/ac1024's own
# documented reason (`protocol-framing-magic-fixed-8-bytes`): `Framing::Magic` unconditionally
# reads/compares EXACTLY 8 raw bytes regardless of a shorter declared literal's own width — ply's
# real magic is only 4 bytes ("ply\n"), so `framing magic 0x706c790a` would incorrectly demand 8.
framing record
header fixed 4
field magic fixed 4
chain body bytes
